<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    ONE KEY, AND A PROVISIONING PROFILE THAT AUTHORIZES IT. Both halves are required
    and neither is obvious, which is how this file spent a week empty while the device
    signing key was never stored at all (myne-md/myne#959).

    `com.apple.application-identifier` is the app's identity, and every keychain write
    that carries an access control needs one. Without it macOS answers
    `errSecMissingEntitlement` (-34018) and the item is not written: the device key fell
    back to the person key, every signed head was format `0x02`, and sync demanded the
    identity phrase again after each unlock with nothing but a prompt to say so.
    Measured on macOS 26 with a Developer ID signature and no profile: both the data
    protection keychain AND the classic one refuse the ACL write, and neither
    `com.apple.developer.team-identifier` nor `keychain-access-groups` changes that.
    With this one key and a profile embedded, the same write succeeds and the item
    reports `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly`, which is what
    `core/src/plugins/sync/device_identity.rs` asks for.

    `com.apple.developer.team-identifier` is deliberately absent: measured alongside,
    it changes nothing once the application identifier is present.

    `keychain-access-groups` is deliberately absent too. It was the only key here
    before 2026-10-26 and it was removed because it granted nothing: it is the app's
    default group, and the quick-unlock provider never asks for a group. The removal
    was still wrong, because it took the application identifier with it and that one
    is load-bearing. Do not re-add the group; do not remove the key above.

    On macOS 26 a restricted entitlement is honored ONLY when the bundle embeds a
    provisioning profile that authorizes it, so `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json` must keep
    `bundle.macOS.files` pointing at that profile. The two failure modes are a trap and
    each looks like something else: entitlement without an embedded profile and the app
    cannot launch at all (`Launchd job spawn failed`, NSPOSIXErrorDomain 163, while a
    `codesign` verification still passes), profile embedded and entitlement absent and every
    ACL write returns -34018 with the UI silently falling back. Procedure and the
    profile's own generation steps: `myne-ops/runbooks/local-multiplatform-build.md`.

    Never write a double hyphen inside this comment: an XML comment may not contain one,
    `plutil -lint` accepts it anyway, and AMFI's parser does not, so `codesign` reports
    `AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error` and then signs WITHOUT the entitlement while still
    exiting 0. The symptom is the -34018 above, which is why this cost a round.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
  <string>D8UWYY2KY7.md.myne.desktop</string>
</dict>
</plist>
